WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.300
-Giancarlo welcome to the show.

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.567
-Thank you, thank you.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:06.534
I have so many questions
to ask you.

00:00:06.534 --> 00:00:08.767
I have so many things
that I'm a fan of yours.

00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:10.801
Thank you so much
for coming on the show.

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:13.000
I'm honored to have you here.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.434
I want to start off by saying --
"Mandalorian," we want it back.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:19.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.868 --> 00:00:22.434
You're fantastic in it.
It is back in March.

00:00:22.434 --> 00:00:25.934
Finally, it's coming.
-"You have something I want."

00:00:25.934 --> 00:00:28.634
-Aah! That's exactly --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:30.000
-And you know what?
I gotta tell you,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.234
thank you so much
for having me on the show.

00:00:31.234 --> 00:00:33.801
I'm honored to be here.
You are the king.

00:00:33.801 --> 00:00:35.234
I've wanted to be here
for many years.

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:37.567
And finally, you invited me!
-Oh, yeah!

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:39.567
That's what I'm talking about.
Come on, bud.

00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:42.000
-"Mandalorian" is a great show.
I really love doing that show.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.801
-But you know how to do it.
You know how to create

00:00:43.801 --> 00:00:46.200
a character and make it
a legendary character.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.167
Here you are, though, acting
opposite a very cute puppet.

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:51.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.968
-I gotta tell you, I will squash
that little big-eared bastard.

00:00:54.968 --> 00:00:56.601
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, that's exactly right!

00:00:56.601 --> 00:00:58.100
Yes.
-Look, I walk by that puppet --

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.501
Jon Favreau,
who called me and said,

00:00:59.501 --> 00:01:00.734
"Do you want to be
a part this show?"

00:01:00.734 --> 00:01:01.934
He said, "I have good news,
I have bad news."

00:01:01.934 --> 00:01:03.167
I said, "Well,
tell me the good news first."

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:04.901
"The good news is,
I wrote a role for you

00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:06.567
of Moff Gideon,
in 'Mandalorian.'"

00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:08.767
I said, "What's that?"
He said, "it's a 'Star Wars.'

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:10.434
It's a new 'Star Wars' show
that I'm doing."

00:01:10.434 --> 00:01:12.634
I said, "Okay, great.
What's the bad news?"

00:01:12.634 --> 00:01:16.200
"The bad news is that all the
money is going into the Volume."

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.567
And he started
talking technically

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.400
and talking about the story
and all these good things.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.000
And I said to him, "Jon, I want
to work with people I like.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.467
That's the bottom line."
I did not want to say --

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.934
came very close to saying --
"I would do it for free."

00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:28.367
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:29.667
No, you don't to say that.

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:31.734
-No, but you loved it that much.
-I loved it that much.

00:01:31.734 --> 00:01:33.767
And he said,
"Are there any requests?"

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:36.200
I said, "I have one request.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.167
Please, I must have a cape."

00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:41.167
And he said, "Done deal!"
-Wow!

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.734
-And that --
-That was you.

00:01:42.734 --> 00:01:45.033
-It was me.
It sold me on everything

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:46.901
that I could have a cape.
Because, you know,

00:01:46.901 --> 00:01:50.767
this show allowed me to feel
like a kid again.

00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:52.901
You know?
It made me dream again.

00:01:52.901 --> 00:01:55.434
It made me be in wonder again.
It made me feel like I could

00:01:55.434 --> 00:01:59.167
go any place in the universe,
and carry my dreams,

00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:01.234
my hopes,
and my imagination with me.

00:02:01.234 --> 00:02:03.801
-Wow. I kind of feel
that way watching it.

00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:05.901
That's a great way
of putting it.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:07.934
Really.
It really is one of the most

00:02:07.934 --> 00:02:10.200
creative shows to watch that.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.334
And to actually watch
Grogu come to life.

00:02:13.334 --> 00:02:15.534
What did you call it
before it had a name?

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:19.334
-Well, it was called The Child
for a long time.

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:21.701
And it was sitting on a desk.
And I'd walk by,

00:02:21.701 --> 00:02:23.434
and Jon and I were chatting --
I think he's a genius.

00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:24.634
He's written
such a beautiful show.

00:02:24.634 --> 00:02:26.901
David Filoni, his partner,
is also fantastic.

00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:28.234
This comes out of a relationship

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:31.133
with George Lucas
and Joseph Campbell.

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:33.968
So, what we love about the show,
you may not know, is that

00:02:33.968 --> 00:02:36.534
it's the power of the mythology
of the show

00:02:36.534 --> 00:02:40.267
that you can see your hero and
your heroine within Mando.

00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:42.567
And we walk by a table,
and he said, "Hey,"

00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:47.067
just casually, "You want to take
a look at The Child?"

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:49.834
I said, "Certainly."
And he pulls the canvas off.

00:02:49.834 --> 00:02:53.467
And I looked at
this practical child.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:54.667
And he explained to me
it was gonna be this,

00:02:54.667 --> 00:02:55.667
it was going to be that -- CGI.

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:56.934
I said, "No, this perfect."

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:59.167
And then the puppeteer walked by
at that very moment.

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:02.634
And the puppeteer said, "Do you
want to see and talk to him?"

00:03:02.634 --> 00:03:04.033
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:08.667
I went, "Okay."
And The Child opened its eyes,

00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:10.734
turned its head,
wiggled its ears.

00:03:10.734 --> 00:03:14.033
And I was -- you can't take your
eyes off of this child.

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:17.400
Grogu is fascinating,
mysterious, intelligent, smart.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.934
And I looked at Jon, and I said,
"Jon --"

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:21.467
I said, "Jon,
this is the star of the show."

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.400
And he stood back,
and he scratched his chin,

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.801
and he said, "You think so?"
I said, "I know so.

00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:30.434
And every scene that I'm in
has to be with this child."

00:03:30.434 --> 00:03:31.767
[ Laughter ]

00:03:31.767 --> 00:03:33.534
Put it in the contract, baby!

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:34.667
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:36.400
-Exactly.
And you crush it.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.400
But then, everyone loves you.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.901
We love your character,
even though you're the villain.

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:45.434
But just -- also, online,
a lot of fans want you to be

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:48.501
in the
Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:03:48.501 --> 00:03:52.734
-I love what Marvel does.
I love that the fans can see me

00:03:52.734 --> 00:03:55.033
in this universe
and are proposing not only

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:58.567
one character, possibly Magneto,
possibly freeze.

00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:03.067
And the biggest ask
is Charles Xavier.

00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:05.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:05.334 --> 00:04:07.400
But, look,
I want to play a good guy.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.033
And Charles is smart.
He's good.

00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:13.601
But I feel as if there may be
some mileage with me and Marvel.

00:04:13.601 --> 00:04:16.901
I know I love what they do.
I know I love creativity.

00:04:16.901 --> 00:04:19.601
So, who knows what could happen.
Keep putting it out there.

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:23.367
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:25.067
The fans love you.

00:04:25.067 --> 00:04:27.267
I heard a rumor, and
tell me if this is true or not.

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:29.634
That you may or may not have
something to do with

00:04:29.634 --> 00:04:32.200
Barack and Michelle Obama's
relationship.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.601
-Oh, my goodness.
So, I love what Barack Obama

00:04:34.601 --> 00:04:38.200
has done and continues to do
to forward the human spirit

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.834
and also to exemplify someone

00:04:40.834 --> 00:04:44.367
who has a wonderful morality
and who is in service.

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:45.834
I believe that to be in service

00:04:45.834 --> 00:04:48.801
is our best bet
for our own life.

00:04:48.801 --> 00:04:52.033
So, I was at
the Democratic convention.

00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:57.000
And I was standing
about from here to the monitor

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.367
from Barack
when he made his speech.

00:04:58.367 --> 00:05:00.133
And I was so very impressed.

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:01.901
I was with Robert F. Kennedy Jr.
We walked out.

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.200
He said,
"I'm gonna get the car."

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.634
And I said,
"Okay, I'll wait for you."

00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:09.200
And I'm walking,
and I hear, "Yo!"

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.300
And I'm like,
"Who is yo'ing me?"

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:12.734
-You're like,
"Sylvester Stallone is here."

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:15.033
-You know what I mean?
I'm not in the neighborhood.

00:05:15.033 --> 00:05:17.767
-Yeah.
-You know, "Yo!" again.

00:05:17.767 --> 00:05:20.834
And I turn,
and I see these two gentlemen.

00:05:20.834 --> 00:05:22.434
And as they start
coming towards me,

00:05:22.434 --> 00:05:23.734
I realize one is Barack Obama.

00:05:23.734 --> 00:05:25.167
I'm like,
"Well, you've to be kidding me."

00:05:25.167 --> 00:05:26.968
He's like, "I just gotta talk
to you, I gotta talk to you.

00:05:26.968 --> 00:05:28.400
'Do the Right Thing'
is one of my favorite movies."

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.300
Tells me a whole story about how
he bought Michelle ice cream

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.334
and how they went to see
"Do the Right Thing"

00:05:33.334 --> 00:05:36.167
for their very first date.

00:05:36.167 --> 00:05:37.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:41.801
And so, I saw the future.

00:05:41.801 --> 00:05:44.634
And I saw that he was just
a beautiful human being

00:05:44.634 --> 00:05:46.033
and knew who he'd be.
I finally got a chance

00:05:46.033 --> 00:05:47.300
to tell him,
"You're gonna be

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:48.567
the president of
the United States one day.

00:05:48.567 --> 00:05:50.367
Just stay the course."
-You said that to him?

00:05:50.367 --> 00:05:51.934
-Of course I did.
-Yeah, of course you did.

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:54.634
That's exactly the way to do it.
That's exactly right.

00:05:54.634 --> 00:05:56.767
You knew it!
-I knew it!

00:05:56.767 --> 00:06:01.501
-I want to talk about this
new series, "Kaleidoscope."

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:04.033
Before we can get into
what it's about,

00:06:04.033 --> 00:06:07.601
I like the idea that's on top
of the idea, that --

00:06:07.601 --> 00:06:09.200
and tell me if
I've got this correct.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.200
Every Netflix account
is set up differently.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:13.801
Some have different algorithms
or whatever that.

00:06:13.801 --> 00:06:17.300
So when I watch "Kaleidoscope,"
I will start

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:21.434
at a different episode than
if you watch "Kaleidoscope."

00:06:21.434 --> 00:06:23.234
It's just -- the episodes
are all different.

00:06:23.234 --> 00:06:24.567
-Correct.
Correct.

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:26.234
One of the fascinating things
about "Kaleidoscope,"

00:06:26.234 --> 00:06:28.968
which I'm very, very proud of,
is that the delivery system

00:06:28.968 --> 00:06:31.234
is that you can choose
how you watch it,

00:06:31.234 --> 00:06:33.300
which means you're choosing
how the story unfolds.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.968
There are no numbers --
one, two, three, four, five.

00:06:36.968 --> 00:06:41.234
There are only colors -- orange,
blue, green, yellow, white.

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:44.000
It is violet.
And so, it's fascinating.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.334
It's a kaleidoscope of color,
thus the title that ties us in,

00:06:48.334 --> 00:06:51.501
but the most wonderful thing
is that the story is connected

00:06:51.501 --> 00:06:53.400
via character and event.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.901
One event
surrounds all eight episodes.

00:06:55.901 --> 00:06:58.734
You should watch white last,
I suggest.

00:06:58.734 --> 00:07:00.534
And it spans 25 years.

00:07:00.534 --> 00:07:03.300
And I play a character named
Ray Vernon in the beginning,

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.067
who is a master thief.

00:07:05.067 --> 00:07:06.534
And if I told you
how I prepared for this role,

00:07:06.534 --> 00:07:08.868
you'd be excited, too.
Mm.

00:07:08.868 --> 00:07:12.167
So, I play this master thief,
who gives up one life,

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:13.767
gives up one identity,
and becomes another

00:07:13.767 --> 00:07:15.968
through circumstance
and events of the show.

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:20.567
What really drew me to the show,
was that you, as an audience,

00:07:20.567 --> 00:07:22.400
I, as an audience,
would have a choice

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.834
as to how I wanted
to see it happen.

00:07:24.834 --> 00:07:26.400
And there are all these
Easter eggs and clues

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.400
throughout all
that connect every story.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.434
So, you can see
one complete heist.

00:07:31.434 --> 00:07:35.133
It's about a heist that
took place during a hurricane

00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:37.400
back in the late '70s,
early '80s,

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:41.667
of $70 billion
in unsecured bearer bonds.

00:07:41.667 --> 00:07:43.234
And because of
this particular heist

00:07:43.234 --> 00:07:46.367
that happened in history --
we're loosely based on that --

00:07:46.367 --> 00:07:48.934
the U.S. government
changed the law.

00:07:48.934 --> 00:07:52.000
There can be
no more unsecured bonds.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.467
So, since then,
all bonds have to be registered

00:07:55.467 --> 00:07:57.701
so you can't use them
for illicit means,

00:07:57.701 --> 00:08:00.701
i.e, buying arms or drugs
or jewelry.

00:08:00.701 --> 00:08:03.467
So, this particular heist
is an exciting one.

00:08:03.467 --> 00:08:07.667
But it's more exciting
because it pits a ne'er-do-well

00:08:07.667 --> 00:08:12.634
group of thieves
against a large industry,

00:08:12.634 --> 00:08:17.400
a private firm, that will hold
your money and your jewelry.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.167
And it's also a revenge story.
It's a fabulous piece.

00:08:20.167 --> 00:08:23.000
I had a chance
to really excel in,

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.000
physically and also mentally,
my preparation.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.267
I was in an airport,
and I thought to myself,

00:08:27.267 --> 00:08:29.300
"I'm gonna play a thief.
How do I sneak --"

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.200
No, come on, no kidding!
How do I sneak up on somebody

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:35.067
stealthily -- be behind them
without them knowing."

00:08:35.067 --> 00:08:36.067
This really happened.

00:08:36.067 --> 00:08:37.234
I'll tell you the truth
about me.

00:08:37.234 --> 00:08:41.234
I am a bad man.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:41.234 --> 00:08:44.067
So, I sneak up on a lady.
She's walking.

00:08:44.067 --> 00:08:45.400
And everyone's
on their cellphone.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:47.534
No one's paying attention
anymore anyway.

00:08:47.534 --> 00:08:50.033
And she had a pen
in her coat pocket.

00:08:50.033 --> 00:08:52.834
And her coat was open, and
it was flapping a little bit,

00:08:52.834 --> 00:08:55.534
and I thought,
"Let's see how good I am.

00:08:55.534 --> 00:09:00.667
If I can just get to that pen
with two fingers..."

00:09:00.667 --> 00:09:01.934
And so I snuck up on her.

00:09:01.934 --> 00:09:04.400
I was over her right shoulder,
and I trailed her.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.100
And I got my fingers
in her pocket,

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.501
very much
like this patch pocket.

00:09:08.501 --> 00:09:10.200
And I took the pen,
and I slid it up,

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.734
and then I dropped it
on the ground.

00:09:11.734 --> 00:09:13.067
-Yes.
-I didn't want to steal it.

00:09:13.067 --> 00:09:16.033
-No, point proven.
-Point proven! I did it!

00:09:16.033 --> 00:09:18.133
-Point made! I can do this!
-And you know what I did?

00:09:18.133 --> 00:09:19.567
-What?
Oh, wait.

00:09:19.567 --> 00:09:20.701
I think I know what you did.
-I made the "Malcolm X" move.

00:09:20.701 --> 00:09:22.000
What did I do?
Tell me what I did.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.601
-You told her, "Excuse me, miss,
you dropped your pen."

00:09:23.601 --> 00:09:25.200
-That's right, baby!
-That's exactly --

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:27.567 --> 00:09:31.701
That's exactly --
Come on, bud.

00:09:31.701 --> 00:09:34.634
-I was so proud of myself!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:34.634 --> 00:09:36.467
Oh, man.
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:09:36.467 --> 00:09:39.434
Here's Giancarlo Esposito
in "Kaleidoscope."

00:09:39.434 --> 00:09:41.100
Take a look at this.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.767
♪♪

00:09:43.767 --> 00:09:45.634
[ Metal clatter ]

00:09:45.634 --> 00:09:55.634
♪♪

00:09:55.634 --> 00:10:03.367
♪♪

00:10:03.367 --> 00:10:10.133
-♪ Oh, oh, oh, oh ♪

00:10:10.133 --> 00:10:14.200
-No one can pinpoint the exact
moment it all fell apart.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:15.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:15.701 --> 00:10:18.234
-Giancarlo Esposito!

00:10:18.234 --> 00:10:21.434
"Kaleidoscope" is streaming now
on Netflix.

00:10:21.434 --> 00:10:23.801
We'll be right back
with a performance from Alvvays.

00:10:23.801 --> 00:10:25.300
Stick around, everybody.

